// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `v` in a call to `f`
// A COMPILER CRASH, PINNED AS THE LOUD REJECT IT SHOULD ALWAYS HAVE BEEN.
//
// `{ g: T[] }` — an inline shape over a FuncDecl TYPE PARAMETER. The generic original
// declares no concrete struct, exactly as a generic ALIAS declares none, so its shape must
// not be interned: the instances carry pinned concrete names and it is those that reach
// the type section.
//
// The ARRAY annotation classifier has known this since #1218 (`collectA`'s `genTyParamArr`
// arm skips a bare `T[]` param). The SHAPE FIELD classifier did not, so `internInlineShapeTy`
// resolved the field's element through the NOMINAL NAME PATH — and a type parameter is not a
// nominal name. Two outcomes, neither a diagnostic:
//
//   ·  no type named `T` in scope (this file): the element resolved to "", the field's
//      ref-list slot was never interned, and `pushFieldStorage` read `rlWrapIdx[-1]` —
//      an out-of-bounds CRASH of the compiler, under `vl check --codegen` too. That read
//      is written to fail loudly ("ref-list field element type is not interned") and the
//      -1 slipped past its `refSlot >= rlWrapIdx.length` bound on the low side.
//   ·  a REAL `type T` also in scope: the element resolved to THAT UNRELATED TYPE and the
//      field got its wrapper. `vl check --codegen` exited 0 and `vl run` reported invalid
//      wasm. (That SHADOW half stays as it is — `genTyParamArr`'s own header rules a
//      parameter naming a real type binds nothing, and closing it is a separate slice.)
//
// ORDERING WAS THE WHOLE OF THE BUG. Both classifier arms were already written; the
// registry they consult, `genTyParamNames`, was gathered INSIDE `collectA` — which runs
// AFTER `collectS`, the pass that interns shapes. So at intern time the list was empty and
// every membership test short-circuited to false. The gather is now its own pass
// (`collectTyParams`) ahead of `collectU`/`collectS`, declared in the pass table.
//
// The reject is `monomorphize`'s: an inline-shape argument is not a supported generic
// argument type. Whether it SHOULD be supported is a capability question, not this fix —
// what this file pins is that the answer is a diagnostic and never a crash.
function f<T>(v: { g: T[] }): i32 {
  v.g.length
}

print(f({ g: [1, 2, 3] }))
